---
import Button from "../components/Button.astro";
import Container from "../components/Container.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import SectionHeader from "../components/SectionHeader.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="AI & Workers — Syntila"
  description="Connect ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, coding agents, and local models to your Syntila Spaces. Workers run on your own computer with the AI tools you already use."
>
  <Section>
    <Container>
      <SectionHeader
        eyebrow="AI & Workers"
        title="Connect and share your favorite AI models."
        description="A Worker is an AI tool on your computer that a Space is allowed to use. You install the tool and sign in with your own account; Syntila Workspace runs it when someone in the Space asks."
        headingLevel={1}
      />

      <div class="cards worker-catalog" data-site-event="section.workers_reached">
        <article class="worker-card">
          <div class="worker-card__top">
            <h2>ChatGPT</h2>
            <span class="worker-status-badge">Supported</span>
          </div>
          <p>Runs through OpenAI's Codex CLI. Good for reasoning, writing, summaries, and code.</p>
          <p class="worker-card__needs"><strong>You need:</strong> Codex CLI installed and signed in.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="worker-card">
          <div class="worker-card__top">
            <h2>Claude</h2>
            <span class="worker-status-badge">Supported</span>
          </div>
          <p>Runs through Anthropic's Claude Code. Good for long documents, planning, code changes, and review.</p>
          <p class="worker-card__needs"><strong>You need:</strong> Claude Code installed and signed in.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="worker-card">
          <div class="worker-card__top">
            <h2>Google Gemini</h2>
            <span class="worker-status-badge">Supported</span>
          </div>
          <p>Runs through Google's Antigravity CLI. Good for fast research, large inputs, and a second point of view.</p>
          <p class="worker-card__needs"><strong>You need:</strong> Antigravity CLI installed and signed in.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="worker-card">
          <div class="worker-card__top">
            <h2>GitHub Copilot CLI</h2>
            <span class="worker-status-badge worker-status-badge--testing">Testing</span>
          </div>
          <p>Repository-aware help and terminal tasks through GitHub Copilot's command-line agent.</p>
          <p class="worker-card__needs"><strong>You need:</strong> Copilot CLI installed and signed in.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="worker-card">
          <div class="worker-card__top">
            <h2>Grok Build CLI</h2>
            <span class="worker-status-badge worker-status-badge--testing">Testing</span>
          </div>
          <p>Reasoning and coding tasks through xAI's Grok Build command-line agent.</p>
          <p class="worker-card__needs"><strong>You need:</strong> Grok Build CLI installed and signed in.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="worker-card">
          <div class="worker-card__top">
            <h2>Custom MCP Workers</h2>
            <span class="worker-status-badge">Supported</span>
          </div>
          <p>Connect a local model, such as one served by Ollama, or your own agent as a Worker over the Model Context Protocol (MCP).</p>
          <p class="worker-card__needs"><strong>You need:</strong> an MCP server that speaks stdio or Streamable HTTP.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p class="status-legend">
        <strong>Supported</strong> Workers are the ones we test and maintain. <strong>Testing</strong> Workers run today but are still being qualified, so expect rough edges.
      </p>

      <div class="security-callout section-block-gap-lg" id="sharing">
        <strong>Check your plan before you share a Worker.</strong>
        <span>Using your own AI subscription for your own requests is the normal case. When other people in a Space use a Worker you connected, your provider's terms decide whether that is allowed. Many personal subscriptions are for one person only; access billed through a provider's API, or a team or business plan, is usually the right fit for shared use. Rules differ between providers and change over time, so read the terms of your own plan first. <a href="/faq/#sharing-subscriptions">More in the FAQ</a></span>
      </div>

      <div class="section-block-gap-lg">
        <SectionHeader
          eyebrow="MCP Servers &amp; Tools"
          title="Give Workers extra tools with MCP servers."
          description="Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers add tools, such as a database or an issue tracker, that a Worker can call. You register them on your own computer and approve what they offer before any Worker can use them."
        />
        <div class="execution-path-strip">
          <div class="execution-path-step">
            <span>01 · Register</span>
            <strong>Add local or remote servers</strong>
            <p>Use <code>syntila mcp add</code> for a server on your computer or <code>syntila mcp add-remote</code> for one reached over HTTPS.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="execution-path-step">
            <span>02 · Approve</span>
            <strong>Review each tool once</strong>
            <p>Syntila records exactly what each tool says it does. If a server later changes a tool, calls are blocked until you review it again.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="execution-path-step">
            <span>03 · Keep it local</span>
            <strong>Separate settings per run</strong>
            <p>Each run gets its own temporary MCP settings. Your normal CLI configuration is not changed, and server tokens stay on your computer.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="execution-path-step">
            <span>04 · Run</span>
            <strong>Under your account</strong>
            <p>Approved servers run as you, on your computer, with output size limits and cleanup when the run ends.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="actions actions--center section-block-gap-lg">
        <Button href="https://syntila.app" label="Open Syntila" event="cta.open_app" />
        <Button href="/downloads/" label="Download Syntila Workspace" variant="secondary" event="navigation.downloads" />
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>
</SiteLayout>
